#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553823 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-2 t+5,\\qquad y=-2 t^{3}+2 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2 (-2)+5=4+5=9 \\\\ y & =-2 (-2)^{3}+2 (-2)=16-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(9,12).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+2}{-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (-2)^{2}+2}{-2}=\\frac{-22}{-2}=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(9,12)$ and the slope $m=11.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-12=11(x-9).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553825 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+1,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+3 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+1=-4+1=-3 \\\\ y & =2 (-2)^{3}+3 (-2)=-16-6=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(-3,-22).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+3}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (-2)^{2}+3}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,-22)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{27}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553830 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4 t-1,\\qquad y=-2 t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4 (1)-1=4-1=3 \\\\ y & =-2 (1)^{3}+5 (1)=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+5}{4}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (1)^{2}+5}{4}=\\frac{-1}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(3,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{1}{4}(x-3).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553839 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-3 t+5,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3 (1)+5=-3+5=2 \\\\ y & =2 (1)^{3}+(1)=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,3).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-3,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+1}{-3}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (1)^{2}+1}{-3}=\\frac{7}{-3}=-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(2,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-3=-\\frac{7}{3}(x-2).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554629 :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\hspace{0.222em}{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554750 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=-{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-9t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}-2t-8,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}-12t-9.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}-12t-9 & =0 \\\\ -3(t+1)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(-1) & =-5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=-1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554758 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-4{t}^{2}-6t,\\,y=-2{t}^{3}+18{t}^{2}-48t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}-8t-6,\\qquad y'(t)=-6{t}^{2}+36t-48.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+36t-48 & =0 \\\\ -6(t-4)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =2,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(2) & =-30\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(4) & =-70\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=2$ and $t=4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554759 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-16t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-18t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)={t}^{2}-16,\\qquad y'(t)=6{t}^{2}-12t-18.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-12t-18 & =0 \\\\ 6(t-3)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =-15\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(3) & =-7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554766 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}+32t,\\,y=-{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+9t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}+32,\\qquad y'(t)=-3{t}^{2}-6t+9.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =0 \\\\ -3(t-1)(t+3) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-3) & =14\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(1) & =30\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-3$ and $t=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554769 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}+12t,\\,y={t}^{3}-\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-6t,\\,-\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if $y'(t)=0$ while $x'(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2{t}^{2}+2t+12,\\qquad y'(t)=3{t}^{2}-3t-6.\n$$\n\nSolving $y'(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-3t-6 & =0 \\\\ 3(t-2)(t+1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x'(-1) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ x'(2) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-1$ and $t=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554827 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨\\sqrt{6},2,-1⟩$ and $b=⟨4,-2,-3⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{6})(4)+(2)(-2)+(-1)(-3)}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}-4+3}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+4\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{29}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+4\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+4\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{-1+4\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{29}}-\\frac{-1+4\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}\\left(-1+4\\sqrt{6}\\right)-\\sqrt{29}\\left(-1+4\\sqrt{6}\\right)}{\\sqrt{319}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554837 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{6},1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,1,\\sqrt{6}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\sqrt{3})(-1)+(-\\sqrt{6})(1)+(1)(\\sqrt{6})}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8}}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{30}-2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{80}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554839 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3},4⟩$ and $b=⟨-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{6},\\sqrt{2}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a+{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(-\\sqrt{2})+(-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{6})+(4)(\\sqrt{2})}{\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{6})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{2}+4\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{20}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a+{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{10}}+\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}+4\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{200}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554842 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨3,\\sqrt{5},1⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-\\sqrt{3},2⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(-1)+(\\sqrt{5})(-\\sqrt{3})+(1)(2)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3-\\sqrt{15}+2}{\\sqrt{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{8}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{15}-1}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =-\\frac{1+\\sqrt{15}}{\\sqrt{8}}-\\left(-\\frac{1+\\sqrt{15}}{\\sqrt{15}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right)-(2\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\sqrt{15}-1\\right))}{\\sqrt{120}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554844 :question/problem "Let $a=⟨\\sqrt{3},3,-\\sqrt{6}⟩$ and $b=⟨-3,-4,-\\sqrt{6}⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})(-3)+(3)(-4)+(-\\sqrt{6})(-\\sqrt{6})}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\sqrt{3}-12+6}{\\sqrt{31}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{31}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{b}_{x}{a}_{x}+{b}_{y}{a}_{y}+{b}_{z}{a}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{6})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6-3\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{18}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}+6}{\\sqrt{31}}-\\left(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}+6}{\\sqrt{18}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right)-(\\sqrt{31}\\left(-6-3\\sqrt{3}\\right))}{\\sqrt{558}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554853 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(4 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 - 4 t^{2},\\quad y=4 t^{2},\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(4\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(4\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(- 8 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(8 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 8 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(8 t\\right)+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 32 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =- 32 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- 32 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[- 16 t^{2} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(-16\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)]-[\\left(-16\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554863 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 6 x + 5 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(5 t^{2} - 5 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t^{3} - 6 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=5 t^{2} - 5 t,\\quad y=6 t^{3} - 6 t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- 6 x + 5 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(5 t^{2} - 5 t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(6 t^{3} - 6 t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(10 t - 5\\right)\\,i+\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(10 t - 5\\right)\\,i+\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(10 t - 5\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(18 t^{2} - 6\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(300 t^{4} - 450 t^{3} + 150 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(10 t - 5\\right) \\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(10 t - 5\\right) \\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[60 t^{5} - \\frac{225 t^{4}}{2} + 50 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(50\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{225}{2}\\right)+\\left(60\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554865 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(3 - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 - 3 t^{2},\\quad y=3 t^{2},\\quad z=4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(3\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(4 \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(3\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(- 6 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(6 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(4 \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(3\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 6 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(6 t\\right)+\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 \\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 18 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- 16 \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =- 18 t - 8 \\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- 18 t - 8 \\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[- 9 t^{2} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(-9\\right)+\\left(4\\right)]-[\\left(-9\\right)+\\left(4\\right)] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554867 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(12 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =12 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(12 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =[\\left(0\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554872 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(2 - 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 - 2 t^{2},\\quad y=2 t^{2},\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- z\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 - 2 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(- 4 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(2\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(- 4 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(4 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2\\right)\\cdot\\left(- 4 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 t\\right)+\\left(- \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\pi \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- \\pi \\sin{\\left(\\pi t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =- 8 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{1}\\left(- 8 t - \\frac{\\pi \\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[- 4 t^{2} + \\frac{\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}}{4}\\right]_{-1}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)]-[\\left(-4\\right)+\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)] \\\\ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554886 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 3 x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(t^{2} - t\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=t^{2} - t,\\quad y=3 t^{3} - 3 t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- 3 x + y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{2} - t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\,i+\\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 t - 1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 t^{4} - 9 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2 t - 1\\right) \\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(2 t - 1\\right) \\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{6 t^{5}}{5} - \\frac{9 t^{4}}{4} + t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{9}{4}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =- \\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554891 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 5 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(3 t^{2} - 3 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(5 t^{3} - 5 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 t^{2} - 3 t,\\quad y=5 t^{3} - 5 t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- 5 x + 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(15 t^{3} - 15 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 t^{2} - 3 t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(5 t^{3} - 5 t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(6 t - 3\\right)\\,i+\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(15 t^{3} - 15 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(6 t - 3\\right)\\,i+\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(15 t^{3} - 15 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(6 t - 3\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(90 t^{4} - 135 t^{3} + 45 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 t - 3\\right) \\left(15 t^{3} - 15 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(6 t - 3\\right) \\left(15 t^{3} - 15 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[18 t^{5} - \\frac{135 t^{4}}{4} + 15 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(15\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{135}{4}\\right)+\\left(18\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =- \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554912 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- x + 4 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(4 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{3} - t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 t^{2} - 4 t,\\quad y=t^{3} - t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- x + 4 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{3} - t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(8 t - 4\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2} - 1\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(8 t - 4\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 t^{2} - 1\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(8 t - 4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(3 t^{2} - 1\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(32 t^{4} - 48 t^{3} + 16 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(8 t - 4\\right) \\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(8 t - 4\\right) \\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\frac{32 t^{5}}{5} - 12 t^{4} + \\frac{16 t^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right)+\\left(-12\\right)+\\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =- \\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554913 :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 5 x + 6 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\left(6 t^{2} - 6 t\\right)\\,i+\\left(5 t^{3} - 5 t\\right)\\,j+\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr=\\int_a^b F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=6 t^{2} - 6 t,\\quad y=5 t^{3} - 5 t,\\quad z=\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\left(- 5 x + 6 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r^{\\prime}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(6 t^{2} - 6 t\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(5 t^{3} - 5 t\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\sin{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k \\\\ & =\\left(12 t - 6\\right)\\,i+\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t) & =\\left(\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(0\\right)\\,j+\\left(0\\right)\\,k\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\left(12 t - 6\\right)\\,i+\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)\\,j+\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right)\\,k\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot\\left(12 t - 6\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(15 t^{2} - 5\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi \\cos{\\left(2 \\pi t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(360 t^{4} - 540 t^{3} + 180 t^{2}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(12 t - 6\\right) \\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ=\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr & =\\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot r^{\\prime}(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(\\left(12 t - 6\\right) \\left(30 t^{3} - 30 t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[72 t^{5} - 135 t^{4} + 60 t^{3}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =[\\left(60\\right)+\\left(-135\\right)+\\left(72\\right)]-[\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(0\\right)] \\\\ & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554915 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-7)^{2}=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-7)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-7)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{3}{2},$ $b=7,$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},7)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554932 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-8)^{2}=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-8)^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-8)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=8,$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{3},8)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554938 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+9)^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}=2?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+9)^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2} & =2 \\\\ (x-(-9))^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2} & =(\\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=-9,$ $b=\\frac{5}{3},$ and $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-9,\\frac{5}{3})$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554939 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $$\n\\displaystyle (x+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=75?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =75 \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\frac{1}{2})^{2} & =(5 \\sqrt{3})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{75}=\\sqrt{25\\cdot 3}=5 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{3}{2},$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $r=5 \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ and its radius is $5 \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554941 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $$\n\\displaystyle (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{3})^{2}=12?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y+\\frac{1}{3})^{2} & =12 \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{9}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right))^{2} & =(2 \\sqrt{3})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=2 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{9}{2},$ $b=- \\frac{1}{3},$ and $r=2 \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{9}{2},- \\frac{1}{3})$ and its radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554943 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=7?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x+\\frac{9}{2})^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =7 \\\\ (x-\\left(- \\frac{9}{2}\\right))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{7})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=- \\frac{9}{2},$ $b=1,$ and $r=\\sqrt{7}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(- \\frac{9}{2},1)$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554946 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $$\n\\displaystyle (x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}=12?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2} & =12 \\\\ (x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+(y-\\frac{5}{3})^{2} & =(2 \\sqrt{3})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{12}=\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}=2 \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{3}{2},$ $b=\\frac{5}{3},$ and $r=2 \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{3})$ and its radius is $2 \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554947 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $(x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=6?$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y+\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =6 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right))^{2} & =(\\sqrt{6})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=3,$ $b=- \\frac{3}{2},$ and $r=\\sqrt{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(3,- \\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554953 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $$\n\\displaystyle (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2}=72?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y+\\frac{7}{2})^{2} & =72 \\\\ (x-\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(y-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right))^{2} & =(6 \\sqrt{2})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{72}=\\sqrt{36\\cdot 2}=6 \\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{5}{3},$ $b=- \\frac{7}{2},$ and $r=6 \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{5}{3},- \\frac{7}{2})$ and its radius is $6 \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554963 :question/problem "What is the center and radius of the circle with equation $$\n\\displaystyle (x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-\\frac{7}{3})^{2}=20?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's compare the general equation to our equation side-by-side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-\\frac{7}{3})^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(y-\\frac{7}{3})^{2} & =(2 \\sqrt{5})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius is the positive square root of the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{20}=\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5}=2 \\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nWe see that $a=\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{7}{3},$ and $r=2 \\sqrt{5}.$ \n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3})$ and its radius is $2 \\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555770 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ between the surfaces $z=0$ and $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/5d/5d8a94cd0df8095c78aec955c3d0f062e38410326399104644b24e981eb1db9a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{25-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555773 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,\\,y-4\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/a6/a6b6faca2cd9cb66d463178a24efeb6f47d1dfa0bffe17c657e0d0be2dd11426.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=y-4=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}zr\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4}^{3}rz\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555811 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,-y-3\\le z\\le 3\\},\n$$\n\nthen $$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}V=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/b1/b1886315cede967bc6fd606cdf7bf9d6ae4f82264ccce22211562aa590a8fe4a.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-y-3=-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4r{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi ,\\,-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le z\\le 3\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({(r\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2}+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}({r}^{2}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+1)\\cdot r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\pi }}}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\int }_{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}-r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -3}}}^{3}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r({r}^{2}+1)}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555819 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ for $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0,$ between the surfaces $z=1$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/14/140ac7f7d6036657a4ba8a0c6944474c2aa0dfba7f7719228cdffb4d0cf9316f.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-quarter of a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=1,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4={r}^{2}+4.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le {r}^{2}+4\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{1}^{{r}^{2}+4}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555841 :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5$ between the surfaces $z=2$ and $z=4+y.$ Express the following triple integral in cylindrical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/28/286910d89e35603dc6558b80b11d06736d213fbad8c9c91825f42ff87b26e318.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=2,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=4+y=4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{5},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\\,2\\le z\\le 4+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{\\mathrm{Δ}}{z}^{2}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}r\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\sqrt{5}}}}{\\int }_{2}^{\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4+r\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }}}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}r{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}z\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}r\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555859 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac28d44b12c5804a36cc9ae7a3655237449bfe5f1933df81d70ece5ee18ed981.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the graph of the straight line above?\n\nI. $y=-4x+2$\n\nII. $y=x+4$\n\nIII. $y=-2x+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's pick two distinct points on the line. For example, $A$ and $B$ with coordinates $(0,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively.\n\n![](images/47/4719dc10bbf4078e6b266a36b50a82e117b3e775395a8a3e44c0dff4ae56f15b.png)\n\nNow, we substitute the coordinates of both points in our equations. If both points satisfy an equation, then we can conclude that the equation represents the line in the graph. Otherwise, if any point does *not* satisfy the equation, then we can conclude that the equation does *not* represent the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation I: $y=-4x+2.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-4(0)+2 \\\\ 4 & =2\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-4x+2$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation II: $y=x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =6\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=x+4$ is *not* equation of the line in the graph.\n\n- Equation III: $y=-2x+4.$\nSubstituting the coordinates of $A(0,4)$ and $B(2,0)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4) & =-2(0)+4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓} \\\\ (0) & =-2(2)+4 \\\\ 0 & =0\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-2x+4$ is an equation of the line in the graph.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equation that represented the line in the graph was $y=-2x+4,$ corresponding to answer choice III. Therefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}]}
